+  WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS. `scope-auditor` FAILed round 1 because `contract.md` asserted the CPO
+  "approved in-thread ('go ahead') after being shown the three proposed fixes" and no matching
+  record existed anywhere. It was right on both counts, and the second is the worse one: the
+  sequence was BACKWARDS. The CPO said "go ahead" to a one-line mention of the issue; the
+  builder described the three-part plan AFTERWARDS and then built it. Writing that up as a
+  ruling given on a plan the CPO had seen is the Appendix A2 pattern — a decision presented as
+  agreed. Corrected before commit.
+
+  THE RULING, verbatim: **"go ahead"** — in answer to "Next up when you want to continue, in
+  order: the alert-deploy bug I found, then the squads issue, then making staging a table."
+  So the authority is: START #61 AS FILED. Nothing narrower, nothing wider.
+
+  WHAT #61 AS FILED CONTAINS, since that is now the whole boundary. It was written by the
+  builder on 2026-08-11 and lists three suggested fixes: (1) derive the apply loop from the
+  file instead of `for i in 0 1`, (2) correct the prose that says the file "holds BOTH
+  policies", (3) "Consider making the apply step genuinely idempotent".
+
+  ITEMS 1 AND 2 ARE THE BUG and are covered outright.
+
+  ITEM 3 IS A BUILDER DECISION and is labelled as one in the contract rather than attributed to
+  the CPO. Reasoning, recorded so it can be attacked: the README currently DOCUMENTS the hazard
+  instead of removing it — it warns that re-running duplicates every policy and that duplicates
+  are "how people learn to ignore alerts". A recipe that must be run carefully is the same
+  class of defect as a count that must be updated carefully, and that class already failed in
+  production. It crosses no §10 line: no new mechanism, no recurring cost, no product, naming
+  or permanent decision. It is separable — item 3 can be reverted without touching 1 and 2.
+
+  EXPLICITLY NOT TAKEN, and reserved to the CPO: turning the apply step into a real script
+  (`scripts/apply_alert_policies.py` or similar). That WOULD be a new operational mechanism. The
+  builder wrote exactly that into the README on the first attempt, contradicting its own
+  contract, and reverted it before review. The recipe stays an inline documented block.
+
+  WHAT THE BUG ACTUALLY WAS, for whoever reads this later: `deploy/nightly/README.md` applied
+  the Cloud Monitoring policies with `for i in 0 1` over a generator that writes one payload per
+  policy declared in `deploy/nightly/alert-policy.json`. The file grew to three. Index 2 was
+  generated and never sent, so `fdp freshness sentinel itself stopped` — the policy that fires
+  when the freshness sentinel itself dies — was never deployed. It FAILS OPEN: the dropped alert
+  is the one whose absence nothing else can reveal. Found 2026-08-11 while deleting the
+  superseded 25h bridge policy, and the two together would have left "nightly and sentinel both
+  dead" with no alert at all. The instance was fixed by hand that day
+  (`alertPolicies/751471063453009885`); this task fixes the RECIPE.
+
+  A SECOND REVIEWER FINDING WORTH KEEPING, because the guard repeated the defect it polices.
+  `platform-reviewer` FAILed round 1: the first version of `tests/test_alert_policy_recipe.py`
+  matched only the SHAPE OF THE RETIRED BASH RECIPE (`for i in 0 1`, `{0..1}`, `polN.json`).
+  The fixed recipe is Python, so all three patterns were already unreachable — `["policies"][:2]`,
+  an `islice(..., 2)` or a `break` would have dropped the third policy with every test green.
+  A guard written against one dead idiom is a literal standing in for the real rule, which is
+  the same defect one level up. Replaced with a STRUCTURAL check: the recipe's Python block is
+  parsed with `ast`, the loop over the declared policies is located, and its iterable must
+  contain no number, no slice, no `islice` and no `break`. Both Python-shaped regressions were
+  then verified to go red.
+
+  ROUND 2 THEN FAILED THAT TOO, correctly, with four rewrites that reproduce #61 while passing
+  every assertion: hoisting the slice into a variable whose name contains "policies", a
+  `continue` on one displayName, a filter baked into a generator expression, and a post-loop
+  DELETE. The AST check only ever inspected the loop's iterable expression and the body's
+  `break`. The docstring also claimed the check "holds for any rewording", which was simply
+  false. THE REAL LESSON, third time in one task: a static check tests how code LOOKS and the
+  defect is about what it DOES. Replaced by EXECUTING the recipe against a stubbed Monitoring
+  API and asserting every declared policy receives a write. All four rewrites verified red.
+
+  ROUND 3 CAP-OVERRIDE — CPO ASKED, ANSWERED, RECORDED BEFORE `review.md` CITES IT.
+  Round 3 FAILed with a finding sharper than either earlier one, and it is worth keeping
